.TH BLASTOFF 1 "2026-10-01" "blastoff 0.1.6" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
blastoff \- manage Starship themes and stored module configurations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B blastoff
.RI [ options ] " command " [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Bash and PowerShell entry points use one Python 3.11+ core.
Stored themes are independent of the active Starship configuration.
Listing never generates presets or writes files.
Welcome, -h, --help and nested help share a branded header and version.
Use the supplied 37-column, 13-row Braille logo when it fits, colored with a
left-to-right purple-to-pink gradient. Narrow, dumb, non-Unicode or redirected
output uses a compact ASCII fallback.
NO_COLOR retains fitting artwork uncolored.
Missing artwork is harmless. No logo scripts or original ANSI files execute.
Help groups commands by task and colors headings/names, with plain descriptions.
All options, aliases and subcommand help remain available.
No discovery, config reads or subprocesses run for welcome/help.
Auto color requires a terminal; NO_COLOR overrides --color always.
Color flags work before or after help, including nested help.
JSON help remains undecorated text; no-command JSON remains a single object.
No Nerd Font or image assets are required.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list, -l, --list
List local themes and built-in presets from starship preset --list.
Local files remain available if Starship is missing or preset discovery fails.
Combined listing returns 0 even when empty; unavailable presets warn on stderr.
JSON listing instead includes preset_discovery with status ok/unavailable and
error null/reason in its single stdout object, without a stderr warning.
Successful empty discovery is distinct from unavailable presets.
Explicit preset requests still fail with status 3 on discovery failure.
.TP
.B theme apply NAME, -t NAME, --theme NAME
Apply a local theme, otherwise a built-in preset.
Use local:NAME or preset:NAME to select an explicit source.
Validate TOML and back up the active configuration before atomic replacement.
.TP
.B theme save NAME
Save the active configuration as a local theme.
.TP
.B theme copy SOURCE NAME
Copy a local theme or preset:NAME to a local theme.
.TP
.B theme import PATH NAME
Import a UTF-8 TOML file from an absolute path.
.TP
.B theme delete NAME --force
Back up and delete a stored theme. The active config remains unchanged.
Refuse deletion when the stored file is the active config path
or its symlink target.
.TP
.B preset list | preset apply NAME | preset save NAME AS
List, apply or save Starship's built-in presets.
.TP
.B module list | module save MODULE [NAME] | module load NAME
Store or replace an explicit TOML table and its descendants.
Dotted paths such as custom.clock are supported.
Loading preserves unrelated configuration values and backs up the old config.
Root dotted-key and inline-table module layouts may be refused safely.
.TP
.B module delete NAME --force
Back up and delete a stored module snippet.
Refuse deletion when the stored file is the active config path
or its symlink target.
.TP
.B backup create | backup list | backup restore ID
Snapshot, list or restore active-config backups.
Restore verifies the snapshot checksum and backs up the current config.
Stored-theme backups can be recovered using theme import.
For stored-module backups, inspect and validate the snapshot before copying it
to a new module file; theme import does not restore module storage.
.TP
.B pick
Choose and apply using fzf, gum or a numbered terminal menu.
Cancellation returns 130 and leaves config/storage unchanged.
Escape or Ctrl-C cancels in fzf/gum. In the line-oriented numbered menu,
use an empty line, Escape then Enter, EOF or Ctrl-C.
Ambient FZF_* and GUM_* settings are ignored by picker children.
Fzf uses an explicit palette, border, source labels and configuration preview.
Alt-P toggles the preview; narrow screens use a lower pane.
Short screens initially hide the preview.
Gum and numbered fallbacks show source labels and instructions without a pane.
The shipped preview helper shows sanitized, bounded TOML, never a live prompt.
No theme commands are executed or active config changed by previewing.
Preset generation uses bounded reads/timeouts and a disposable session cache.
Only trusted preview/toggle bindings are used; no inherited bindings/listeners.
Preview results are snapshots; application re-reads and validates the source.
Failed preset discovery warns on stderr and preserves local choices.
Empty choices return 1 without opening a picker;
invalid numbered input returns 2.
.TP
.B current
Show labeled active path, file/link status and all byte-identical local matches.
Use a purple title and pink labels on capable terminals; no preset guessing.
Missing config and no local match have distinct messages.
.TP
.B doctor
Show version, Python, paths, config presence and executable availability.
Use compact labeled human output, not JSON, by default.
Missing pickers are optional; availability is not tool/config validation.
Neither status command launches tools or writes state.
Both honor NO_COLOR and redirection; --json retains the original machine schema.
Control characters in human path values are escaped.
.TP
.B migrate PATH
Copy valid themes from an absolute legacy directory.
Refuse collisions. Preserve source files and the active configuration.
Migration is per-file atomic; an I/O failure can leave a partial copied set.
.TP
.B completion bash|zsh|fish
Print static shell completion code to stdout. Do not edit shell profiles.
Completion is positional: local source names/prefixes, stored modules, paired
backup filenames, import paths and migration directories. Destination names and
module-save routes remain free text.
No live preset lookup or application launch.
Backup completion does not validate metadata, kind or checksum.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --force
Permit backed-up replacement of a stored file or backed-up deletion.
.TP
.B --replace-link
Replace an active config symlink with a regular file, recording its old link
and contents in a backup. Never modify the link target.
.TP
.B --json
Write undecorated JSON. Success goes to stdout; errors go to stderr.
.TP
.B --color auto|always|never
Color mode. NO_COLOR suppresses color even with always.
.TP
.B --version
Print the semantic version without invoking Starship.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B STARSHIP_CONFIG
Absolute active config path. Defaults to ~/.config/starship.toml.
A nonempty override is honored even when its file does not yet exist.
.TP
.B BLASTOFF_HOME
Absolute storage root. Defaults to ~/.config/blastoff.
Contains themes, modules and backups.
.TP
.B BLASTOFF_PYTHON
Python executable path or command name. Defaults to python3 in Bash.
PowerShell also supports the Windows py launcher.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Suppress terminal decoration.
.SH FILES
~/.config/blastoff/themes/*.toml
.br
~/.config/blastoff/modules/*.toml
.br
~/.config/blastoff/backups/*.{toml,json}
.br
~/.config/starship.toml
.SH SAFETY
The storage root may be a directory symlink or Windows junction.
For example, ~/.config/blastoff may link to ~/dots/config/blastoff.
Resolve that root once, including relative links and linked ancestors;
all link targets must be existing directories (at most 40 redirects).
Leave the link intact; report canonical storage paths in doctor and results.
Ordinary missing storage directories are created only by mutations.
.PP
Refuse non-regular files, unsafe names, linked storage children and leaf files,
and symlink/reparse parents for active-config writes.
A linked .config directory does not authorize config writes through it;
use a real-path STARSHIP_CONFIG override instead.
Installer/profile protections and active-file --replace-link are unchanged.
Cooperating writers through storage aliases share the canonical directory lock.
Locks are not automatically broken; inspect stale locks after interruption.
Files are limited to 4 MiB. Backups are retained until manually removed.
No network, telemetry or prompt rendering.
Application commands do not edit shell startup files;
the public installer may add owned discovery hooks as described below.
.SH INSTALLATION
From reviewed sources, use:
.nf
bash install.sh --dry-run
bash install.sh
bash uninstall.sh --dry-run
bash uninstall.sh
.fi
No action word or --yes is required by these public wrappers.
Use --prefix PATH for an explicit absolute destination.
Defaults are native Termux PREFIX, other POSIX ~/.local,
and Windows LOCALAPPDATA/Programs/Blastoff (fallback ~/.local).
Source checksums are mandatory even for dry-run; never automatically refreshed.
Only after reviewing deliberate source changes, run bash scripts/build.sh.
Plans support --color auto|always|never; NO_COLOR wins.
Dry-run writes nothing and executes no profiles or shell probes.
.PP
Public install prefers native completion and manual discovery directories.
Zsh receives canonical share/zsh/site-functions/_blastoff plus an owned copy in
share/zsh/functions/Completion/Unix/_blastoff when the native directory exists;
Termux's compiled fpath omits site-functions.
Custom prefixes may require minimal owned PATH/MANPATH/completion hooks.
Regular profile encoding and unrelated content are preserved.
Linked profiles/targets are never rewritten; unsafe integration is refused with
manual setup instructions. Fish uses a conf.d drop-in.
When pwsh is available, an owned hook imports the absolute versioned manifest.
Native Windows runtime verification remains pending.
Profiles are never executed automatically; review activation instructions or
start a fresh shell. Bash/Zsh may need hash -r/rehash in an existing session.
.PP
Runtime payloads under share/blastoff/versions are immutable.
Repeated install restores missing support or refreshes unmodified supported
output; automatic downgrades are skipped.
Public install migrates verified legacy ~/.local ownership
when the target differs.
Target commit precedes resumable legacy cleanup; rerun install to finish cleanup
before uninstalling. Modified owned files, unowned collisions and unsafe paths
block changes. Never delete ownership manifests or break locks to force updates.
.PP
Uninstall removes verified owned files and exact owned profile blocks.
It retains user data, unrelated profile edits, empty directories and recovery
backups under the prefix's share/blastoff/profile-backups directory.
Per-file atomic writes do not provide full multi-file/power-loss transactions.
.PP
Compatibility: bash scripts/install.sh install --yes and uninstall --yes remain
supported, as does native scripts/install.ps1 with the same action/flags.
Use --dry-run before changes. Fresh legacy installs leave integration manual
unless already owning it. See docs/INSTALLATION.md for manual activation.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 success; 1 operation failure; 2 usage error;
3 missing dependency or preset failure;
130 cancellation or interruption.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
blastoff --list
blastoff theme apply local:purple
blastoff theme save work
blastoff preset save catppuccin-powerline catppuccin
blastoff module save directory compact-directory
blastoff module load compact-directory
blastoff backup create
blastoff backup restore BACKUP-ID
.fi
